div.p:
Westen blots of whole muscle and calibration curves are shown in Fig. 1 . The basal abundances of HSP72, HSP27, and αβ-crystallin were not significantly different in muscle between younger and older adults (p > 0.05, Fig. 1C, 1D, and 1F). However, both basal pHSP27 Ser15 (∼1.8-fold, Levene’s test F(2,16) = 6.782, p = 0.007; Brown-Forsye and Welch's (F(2,8.391) = 7.082, p = 0.015; Games-Horwell post hoc p = 0.05; 95%CI: –0.92 to 0.04;
η p 2
= 0.43) and pαB-crystallin Ser59 were relatively higher in 4 older individuals and showed significantly different (Levene’s test F(2,16) = 35.2, p < 0.001; Brown-Forsye and Welch’s (F(2,8.391)= 3.9, p = 0.05; Games-Horwell’s post hoc p = 0.06) in older compared to younger adults (Fig. 1E and 1G). Compared with basal levels in older adults, HIT had no effect on HSP72, HSP27, or αB-crystallin abundances (Fig. 1C, 1D, and 1F) appreciate multiple comparisons test but reduced pHSP27 Ser15 (∼1.8-fold, p = 0.01; 95%CI: –0.16 to 0.95;
η p 2
= 0.43) and pαB-crystallin Ser59 (∼2.9-fold, p = 0.05, 95%CI: –0.14 to 0.16;
η p 2
= 0.46,)) levels (Fig. 1E and 1G).

Westen blots of pooled single muscle fibers and calibration curves are shown in Fig. 2 . Compared to type II fibers, there were higher basal protein abundances in type I fibers for HSP72 (young only, Levene’s test F(3, 22) = 3.4, p = 0.04, Univariate analysis F(3, 22) = 6.9, p = 0.02; Games-Horwell post hoc p = 0.032; 95%CI: 0.38–0.80;
η p 2
= 0.48; Fig. 2A and 2B), HSP27 (young only, Levene’s test F(3, 22) = 0.15, p = 0.9, Univariate analysis F(3, 22) = 4.57, p = 0.01; Tukey post hoc p = 0.05; 95%CI: –0.03 to 0.55;
η p 2
= 0.38; Fig. 2C and 2D), αB-crystallin (young, Levene’s test F(3, 22) = 4.7, p = 0.01, Univariate analysis F(3, 22) = 8.53, p < 0.01; Games-Horwell post hoc p = 0.04; 95%CI: 0.08–0.97;
η p 2
= 0.54); and old, Games-Horwell post hoc p = 0.01; 95%CI: 0.08–0.60; Fig. 2F and 2G), pHSP27 Ser15 (old only, Levene’s test F(3, 22) = 1.3, p = 0.27, Univariate analysis F(3, 22) = 5.08, p < 0.01; Tukey post hoc p < 0.01; 95%CI: 0.12–0.92;
η p 2
= 0.41; Fig. 2C and 2E), and pαB-crystallin Ser59 (old only, Levene’s test F(3, 22) = 5.6, p = 0.03, Univariate analysis F(3, 22) = 6.18, p = 0.05; Games-Horwell post hoc p = 0.02; 95%CI: 0.11–1.25;
η p 2
= 0.62; Fig. 2F and 2H). There were no differences in the basal abundances between type I and type II fibers for HSP72 and HSP27 in old individuals, pHSP27 Ser15 or pαB-crystallin Ser59 in young individuals, and pHSP27 Ser82 for both young and older individuals (Fig. 2 and Supplementary Fig. 1). Comparing type I or type II fibers between young and old individuals, there were no differences in the abundances of HPSP72, HSP27, or αB-crystallin (Fig. 2B, 2D, and 2G). There was a higher abundance of pHSP27 Ser15 (Games-Horwell post hoc p = 0.04; 95%CI: –0.84 to –0.12; Fig. 2E) and pαB-crystallin Ser59 (Games-Horwell post hoc p = 0.02; 95%CI: –0.97 to 1.25; Fig. 2H) in type I fibers from older compared with younger adults; however, there was no difference in type II fibers.
